=== p2-windows · W7 · page:system/DBHealth (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:system/DBHealth   kind=page   web route=/db-health
Web source  : web/src/features/system/pages/DBHealthPage.tsx
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client feed), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the sibling W7
  ApiLogsPage / FeedbackQueuePage / SchemaDriftPage do. The generated prompt's idealized
  "DBHealthPage.xaml + DBHealthPageViewModel.cs under TeslaSync/Features/System" is mapped onto these real
  conventions. The surface registers itself with the W4 ShellPageFactory by route name ("DBHealthDashboard",
  already in RouteTable.cs → /db-health, group Diagnostics).
- Namespace: TeslaSync.App.FeatureViews.Diagnostics (the page lives in the Diagnostics nav group). The literal web
  folder name "system" maps to a C# namespace ending in `.System`, which collides with the BCL `System` root inside
  the namespace body (FeatureViews.System would shadow global System for inline-qualified references); the repo's
  own "system" page (StatusPageSkeleton) likewise uses a disambiguated namespace (SystemStatus). `.Diagnostics` is
  the route-group-accurate, collision-free choice; the ledger unitId stays page:system/DBHealth.
- Data binding (ADR-004): the three manifest data sources are useDBStats (GET /dev-tools/db-stats), useMigrations
  (GET /dev-tools/migration-status) and useConnectionPool (GET /dev-tools/runtime-info), none parameterized. The
  view-model reads through an injected IDbHealthFeed; the default EmptyDbHealthFeed resolves the empty states, and
  the generated-client-backed DbHealthClientFeed (DBHealthPage.Source.cs) binds to the generated OpenAPI operations
  get_api_v1_dev_tools_db_stats / _migration_status / _runtime_info, asserted in tests via FakeApiClient. No HTTP
  touches the view. Each source has an independent loading/error/data state; a stats or migration failure raises the
  top "Error loading data" banner (web queryError = statsError || migrationError); a pool failure renders the
  "no pool data" empty state with no banner (web useConnectionPool surfaces no page-level error).
- The Go wire shape is minimal (db-stats → tables[{schema,name,row_count}] + table_count + database_size;
  migration-status → {version,dirty}; runtime-info → {…,max_open,open,in_use,idle,wait_count}); size_bytes,
  index_count, last_vacuum, pending, migrations[], current_version and wait_duration_ms are NOT returned, so every
  web null-safe branch (em-dash size/indexes/last-vacuum, the >100MB large-table highlight, the no-migration-history
  empty state, waitDurationMs ?? 0) is reproduced via tolerant snake_case parsers (DbStatsSnapshot/MigrationSnapshot/
  PoolSnapshot.FromJson + JsonReadHelpers).
- The web page renders all states INSIDE the page body (per-region), so the native port reproduces them as visible
  Fluent regions per ADR-006: per-region loading → TsSkeleton / TsChartContainer Loading; error → top
  TsAlertBanner(Danger); empty → TsEmptyState / TsChartContainer Empty / TsDataTable EmptyMessage; success → the
  populated stat cards, bar chart, table and panels. Never a blank region.
- SI units: the only unit-bearing display is bytes (DB size / table size), formatted at the display boundary by the
  page-local FormatBytes (a 1:1 port of the web page-local formatBytes: B exact, KB/MB one decimal, GB two decimals).
  Row/pool counts format through the shared NumberFormatting port (web fmtInt); timestamps (recent migrations /
  last vacuum, never populated by the current backend but implemented for fidelity) through DateTimeFormatting.
- All 41 i18n keys resolve through the ILocalizer facade with the exact web key names. 38 already existed in
  Strings/en/Resources.resw (auto-imported from web en.json); the 3 keys that exist only as inline t() defaults in
  the web source (dbHealth.chartTitle.aria, dbHealth.noMigrationData, dbHealth.noPoolData) were added verbatim,
  alphabetically placed. The ar/he catalogs lag en by design; the app falls back to the English default via
  ILocalizer until translated.
- An auto-refresh DispatcherQueueTimer (30 s) mirrors the web INTERVALS.STANDARD refetch so the "Auto-refresh 30s"
  indicator is truthful; the table-sort control (Size / Rows / Name) drives the view-model's sort key, which
  re-projects the sorted rows (web setSortKey + sortedTables useMemo).

